{
  "opening": "This release introduces several new utilities and unifies the behavior of our caching decorators to provide a more consistent developer experience. We are also addressing specific edge cases in argument handling and error propagation to ensure robustness across Python 3.9 and earlier versions.",
  "requirements": [
    {
      "id": "m01",
      "text": "The `has_path` function must return a boolean indicating whether a specified path exists within a nested collection, supporting the same path syntax as `get_in` and `update_in`. It should handle empty paths correctly by returning true if the root object is present."
    },
    {
      "id": "m04",
      "text": "The `update_in` function must correctly process operations on collections where the target path is empty, ensuring no errors are raised when updating the root element directly."
    },
    {
      "id": "m07",
      "text": "Both `@memoize` and `@cache` decorators must expose `.memory`, `.invalidate`, and `.invalidate_all` attributes with identical behavior. Raising `skip` from within a decorated function must halt execution and prevent caching for that call, regardless of which decorator is used."
    },
    {
      "id": "m09",
      "text": "The `@reraise` decorator and context manager must accept a callable or exception type as an argument to specify the target exception for re-raising. When used as a decorator, it must properly pass the original exception information to the fallback logic or re-raise mechanism."
    },
    {
      "id": "m13",
      "text": "Decorators created via `@decorator` must correctly handle functions that have keyword-only arguments, allowing them to be called without providing those arguments if they have defaults or are optional in the underlying function signature."
    },
    {
      "id": "m14",
      "text": "The `rcurry` and `rpartial` functions must correctly preserve and apply keyword arguments when partially applying functions, ensuring that kwargs are not lost or misaligned during the currying process."
    },
    {
      "id": "m16",
      "text": "The `@throttle` decorator (exposed as `limit_error_rate`) must correctly interpret `timedelta` objects passed as the interval argument, ensuring that rate limiting respects the duration specified rather than treating it incorrectly."
    }
  ],
  "closing": "Please verify these behaviors against the public API signatures and ensure backward compatibility is maintained for existing users."
}
